\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Physics is the label.} \textbf  {a)} Materials data fixes quantities that a stated claim must respect: which $2\theta $ can carry a reflection, through Bragg's law and the reference pattern of the named phase; the field of view, through the calibration a scale bar sets; whether a claimed spectral peak coincides with a genuine local maximum of sufficient prominence; and whether a composition lies within tolerance of the computed convex hull. Each constraint is checkable with no human annotation. \textbf  {b)} A chain is parsed into its checkable claims and each claim is tested against the oracle for its own constraint type, returning a per-claim verdict rather than a single score for the answer; detected violations are returned to the model by Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, which is applied to the diffraction and spectra constraint types only (Methods). The chain and the values shown are schematic and illustrate the checks; they are not a model output. \textbf  {c)} The violations these oracles flag are legible physical mistakes, not parsing artifacts. An open-ended categorization of every human-confirmed violation, shown here for the two image oracles that read a quantity off a plot or micrograph, gives a small taxonomy: diffraction failures are dominated by misreading the gridlines or the axis scale, and microscopy failures by scale-bar unit and unit-conversion errors, rather than by errors in the arithmetic that follows. Percentages are shares of the 78 and 93 confirmed violations respectively (Methods); the same categorization for spectra and the three DFT families, on their final-oracle audits, is in Supplementary Table~S5.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{5}{\justifying \textbf {Physics is the label.} \textbf {a)} Materials data fixes quantities that a stated claim must respect: which $2\theta $ can carry a reflection, through Bragg's law and the reference pattern of the named phase; the field of view, through the calibration a scale bar sets; whether a claimed spectral peak coincides with a genuine local maximum of sufficient prominence; and whether a composition lies within tolerance of the computed convex hull. Each constraint is checkable with no human annotation. \textbf {b)} A chain is parsed into its checkable claims and each claim is tested against the oracle for its own constraint type, returning a per-claim verdict rather than a single score for the answer; detected violations are returned to the model by Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, which is applied to the diffraction and spectra constraint types only (Methods). The chain and the values shown are schematic and illustrate the checks; they are not a model output. \textbf {c)} The violations these oracles flag are legible physical mistakes, not parsing artifacts. \rev {An open-ended categorization of every human-confirmed violation, shown here for the two image oracles that read a quantity off a plot or micrograph,} gives a small taxonomy: diffraction failures are dominated by misreading the gridlines or the axis scale, and microscopy failures by scale-bar unit and unit-conversion errors, rather than by errors in the arithmetic that follows. Percentages are shares of the 78 and 93 confirmed violations respectively \rev {(Methods); the same categorization for spectra and the three DFT families, on their final-oracle audits, is in Supplementary Table~S5}}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Representative inconsistencies identified by the multimodal oracles.} \textbf  {a}, X-ray diffraction cases in which the model-reported $2\theta $ position lies outside the $0.5^\circ $ tolerance around the nearest genuine Bragg reflection. \textbf  {b}, Raman spectroscopy cases in which the model-reported Raman shift exceeds the $5\,\mathrm  {cm}^{-1}$ tolerance relative to the nearest genuine spectral peak. \textbf  {c}, Microscopy cases in which the model-reported feature size is inconsistent with the physical scale indicated by the embedded scale bar, producing discrepancies of approximately $10$--$100\times $. All examples are human-confirmed oracle activations and illustrate representative tolerance, unit-conversion, and scale-factor failure modes.}}{8}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:oracle-examples}{{2}{8}{\justifying \textbf {Representative inconsistencies identified by the multimodal oracles.} \textbf {a}, X-ray diffraction cases in which the model-reported $2\theta $ position lies outside the $0.5^\circ $ tolerance around the nearest genuine Bragg reflection. \textbf {b}, Raman spectroscopy cases in which the model-reported Raman shift exceeds the $5\,\mathrm {cm}^{-1}$ tolerance relative to the nearest genuine spectral peak. \textbf {c}, Microscopy cases in which the model-reported feature size is inconsistent with the physical scale indicated by the embedded scale bar, producing discrepancies of approximately $10$--$100\times $. All examples are human-confirmed oracle activations and illustrate representative tolerance, unit-conversion, and scale-factor failure modes}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Oracle validity and audited precision.} The positive-control false-negative rate is the maximum across three planted violation rates (10\%, 30\%, and 50\%); all three rates yielded 0.0000 for every oracle family, which measures completeness on the injected violation types only. Audited precision is the fraction of oracle-fired cases from Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite outputs that human review confirmed as genuine violations of the stated constraint rather than parsing or oracle artifacts, with Wilson 95\% intervals; $n$ counts decided cases (2 spectrum cases marked unsure are excluded; counting them either way gives 92.3--92.7\%). Every audit was sampled and judged under the final oracle definitions used for all reported results (Methods): spectrum responses were collected once and the fired set re-scored with the final oracle before the audit, and DFT responses were re-collected under the final definitions before the audit; the diffraction and microscopy audits were carried out in July 2026 and the others in August 2026. Sources: NFFA, NFFA-EUROPE; MP, Materials Project. $^{a}$Final stability oracle with the near-hull abstention band: the 50 audited cases with $0<E_{\mathrm  {hull}}\le 0.05$ eV\,atom$^{-1}$ are not scored; under the original rule the audit gave 95.3\% [89.5, 98.0] ($n=107$). Consensus: a second annotator, blind to the first labels, re-labelled a random subsample of 30 audited cases per family stratified by the first annotator's verdict, and the two annotators adjudicated together every case on which they disagreed (24 in total); the consensus precision is weighted back to the audited population by stratum, with 95\% intervals from per-stratum Jeffreys posteriors (30 subsampled cases per family; $^{b}$14, the subsampled stability cases scored under the final oracle). Agreement statistics and the oracle-silent audit are in Supplementary Table~S8. The full audits were performed by one annotator (R.K.).}}{9}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:exp1}{{1}{9}{\justifying \textbf {Oracle validity and audited precision.} The positive-control false-negative rate is the maximum across three planted violation rates (10\%, 30\%, and 50\%); all three rates yielded 0.0000 for every oracle family\rev {, which measures completeness on the injected violation types only}. Audited precision is the fraction of oracle-fired cases from Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite outputs that human review confirmed as genuine violations of the stated constraint rather than parsing or oracle artifacts\rev {, with Wilson 95\% intervals; $n$ counts decided cases (2 spectrum cases marked unsure are excluded; counting them either way gives 92.3--92.7\%). Every audit was sampled and judged under the final oracle definitions used for all reported results (Methods): spectrum responses were collected once and the fired set re-scored with the final oracle before the audit, and DFT responses were re-collected under the final definitions before the audit; the diffraction and microscopy audits were carried out in July 2026 and the others in August 2026. Sources: NFFA, NFFA-EUROPE; MP, Materials Project. $^{a}$Final stability oracle with the near-hull abstention band: the 50 audited cases with $0<E_{\mathrm {hull}}\le 0.05$ eV\,atom$^{-1}$ are not scored; under the original rule the audit gave 95.3\% [89.5, 98.0] ($n=107$). Consensus: a second annotator, blind to the first labels, re-labelled a random subsample of 30 audited cases per family stratified by the first annotator's verdict, and the two annotators adjudicated together every case on which they disagreed (24 in total); the consensus precision is weighted back to the audited population by stratum, with 95\% intervals from per-stratum Jeffreys posteriors (30 subsampled cases per family; $^{b}$14, the subsampled stability cases scored under the final oracle). Agreement statistics and the oracle-silent audit are in Supplementary Table~S8. The full audits were performed by one annotator (R.K.).}}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Main evaluation: Physical-Consistency Rate by constraint type and overall, over the checkable responses among 30 attempted items per (model, constraint-type) cell per seed, $S=3$ seeds pooled per cell (bootstrap 95\% CIs omitted from this compact view; see Supplementary Table~S1 for the full per-cell confidence intervals and checkable counts, Supplementary Table~S2 for coverage and alternative conventions, Supplementary Table~S3 for paired, Holm-corrected model comparisons, and Supplementary Table~S4 for class-balanced DFT rates, and the Methods for why $\rho _t=1-\mathrm  {PCR}$ here). DFT column combines the stability, metallicity, and magnitude constraint types; near-hull stability materials are not scored (Methods; the original rule's values are in Supplementary Table~S7). The first four rows are the originally evaluated models; the next four are the later-generation refresh (Methods, Baselines subsection). $^{\ddagger }$GPT-5.6-Sol is OpenAI's flagship tier, added to test whether it exceeds the balanced-tier GPT-5.6-Terra within the same generation; the overall difference is $+0.002$ (95\% CI $[-0.042,0.046]$), not significant, so the design detects no difference between flagship and balanced tier larger than about 0.07 (Discussion). Bold marks the column maximum; it does not imply a significant difference from the next model.}}{11}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{2}{11}{\justifying Main evaluation: Physical-Consistency Rate by constraint type and overall, \rev {over the checkable responses among 30 attempted items} per (model, constraint-type) cell per seed, $S=3$ seeds pooled per cell (bootstrap 95\% CIs omitted from this compact view; see Supplementary Table~S1 for the full per-cell confidence intervals \rev {and checkable counts, Supplementary Table~S2 for coverage and alternative conventions, Supplementary Table~S3 for paired, Holm-corrected model comparisons, and Supplementary Table~S4 for class-balanced DFT rates}, and the Methods for why $\rho _t=1-\mathrm {PCR}$ here). DFT column combines the stability, metallicity, and magnitude constraint types\rev {; near-hull stability materials are not scored (Methods; the original rule's values are in Supplementary Table~S7)}. The first four rows are the originally evaluated models; the next four are the later-generation refresh (Methods, Baselines subsection). $^{\ddagger }$GPT-5.6-Sol is OpenAI's flagship tier, added to test whether it exceeds the balanced-tier GPT-5.6-Terra within the same generation; \rev {the overall difference is $+0.002$ (95\% CI $[-0.042,0.046]$), not significant, so the design detects no difference between flagship and balanced tier larger than about 0.07} (Discussion). \rev {Bold marks the column maximum; it does not imply a significant difference from the next model.}}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Effect of Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, pooled across diffraction and spectra, $n=30$ items per (model, constraint-type) cell, $S=3$ seeds, $K=2$ rounds. $\Delta $: PCR gain (after minus before); $b$: regressions (before-consistent, after-inconsistent); $c$: fixes (before-inconsistent, after-consistent); $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on $(b,c)$; $p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}$: Holm-Bonferroni-adjusted $p$ over this table's 6-test family (Methods, Statistics paragraph). All four CGSV effects survive this correction; both controls remain non-significant. For CGSV, $b=0$ follows from the stopping rule (an item consistent before revision is never revised); the controls revise every item, so their $b$ is not comparable. Paired changes in correctness are in Table~\ref {tab:cgsvacc}.}}{14}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cgsv}{{3}{14}{\justifying Effect of Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, pooled across diffraction and spectra, $n=30$ items per (model, constraint-type) cell, $S=3$ seeds, $K=2$ rounds. $\Delta $: PCR gain (after minus before); $b$: regressions (before-consistent, after-inconsistent); $c$: fixes (before-inconsistent, after-consistent); $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on $(b,c)$; $p_{\mathrm {Holm}}$: Holm-Bonferroni-adjusted $p$ over this table's 6-test family (Methods, Statistics paragraph). All four CGSV effects survive this correction; both controls remain non-significant. \rev {For CGSV, $b=0$ follows from the stopping rule (an item consistent before revision is never revised); the controls revise every item, so their $b$ is not comparable. Paired changes in correctness are in Table~\ref {tab:cgsvacc}.}}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Paired change in answer correctness before and after CGSV, on the same items as Table~\ref {tab:cgsv}. Accuracy uses the non-circular criteria of the accuracy-versus-consistency analysis (diffraction: within $0.1^\circ $ of the nearest reference reflection; spectra: within $2\,\mathrm  {cm}^{-1}$ of the nearest detected local maximum), applied to each item's first-round and final-round claim (accuracy before$\to $after). L/G: items that stop (lost) or start (gained) being accurate; $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on (L, G). Rev.: items the oracle flagged at round 1 and the model revised; of these, A cleared the oracle with an accurate value, Ad cleared it with an admissible value outside the accuracy tolerance, and U were still flagged after $K=2$ rounds.}}{14}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cgsvacc}{{4}{14}{\justifying Paired change in answer correctness before and after CGSV, on the same items as Table~\ref {tab:cgsv}. Accuracy uses the non-circular criteria of the accuracy-versus-consistency analysis (diffraction: within $0.1^\circ $ of the nearest reference reflection; spectra: within $2\,\mathrm {cm}^{-1}$ of the nearest detected local maximum), applied to each item's first-round and final-round claim (accuracy before$\to $after). L/G: items that stop (lost) or start (gained) being accurate; $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on (L, G). Rev.: items the oracle flagged at round 1 and the model revised; of these, A cleared the oracle with an accurate value, Ad cleared it with an admissible value outside the accuracy tolerance, and U were still flagged after $K=2$ rounds}{table.4}{}}
\citation{scirlvr2026}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Physical consistency, oracle-driven correction, and violation detection.} \textbf  {a)} Physical-Consistency Rate (PCR) for nine models across the six label-free constraint types and overall, ordered by overall PCR; shading is linear in PCR. Stability uses the final oracle, which does not score near-hull materials. Cells hold 44--90 checkable records; intervals and coverage are in Supplementary Tables~S1 and S2. $^{\dagger }$Later-generation refresh models; $^{\ddagger }$flagship-tier addition. \textbf  {b)} PCR before and after Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification on identical items, with the two matched controls, self-refinement and equal-compute re-prompting (grey, dashed) (Tables~\ref {tab:cgsv} and~\ref {tab:cgsvacc}). \textbf  {c)} Detector AUC with bootstrap 95\% intervals for the released verifier, two baselines and the six held-out constraint types, on source-disjoint splits (Table~\ref {tab:verifier}).}}{16}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:results}{{3}{16}{\justifying \textbf {Physical consistency, oracle-driven correction, and violation detection.} \textbf {a)} Physical-Consistency Rate (PCR) for nine models across the six label-free constraint types and overall, ordered by overall PCR; shading is linear in PCR. \rev {Stability uses the final oracle, which does not score near-hull materials. Cells hold 44--90 checkable records; intervals and coverage are in Supplementary Tables~S1 and S2.} $^{\dagger }$Later-generation refresh models; $^{\ddagger }$flagship-tier addition. \textbf {b)} PCR before and after Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification on identical items, with the two matched controls, self-refinement and equal-compute re-prompting (grey, dashed) \rev {(Tables~\ref {tab:cgsv} and~\ref {tab:cgsvacc})}. \textbf {c)} Detector AUC with bootstrap 95\% intervals for the released verifier, two baselines and the six held-out constraint types, on source-disjoint splits \rev {(Table~\ref {tab:verifier})}}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Verifier precision/recall/$F_1$ (in-distribution and held-out-constraint-type splits, both source-disjoint) and the detector's bootstrap 95\% CI on ROC AUC, predicting a real oracle violation from held-out verifier scores. Expected calibration error is reported in the text for the in-distribution split (ECE $=0.096$). No pooled across-constraint-type AUC is reported (see text).}}{17}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:verifier}{{5}{17}{\justifying Verifier precision/recall/$F_1$ (in-distribution and held-out-constraint-type splits, both source-disjoint) and the detector's bootstrap 95\% CI on ROC AUC, predicting a real oracle violation from held-out verifier scores. Expected calibration error is reported in the text for the in-distribution split (ECE $=0.096$). No pooled across-constraint-type AUC is reported (see text)}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \justifying  In-distribution verifier performance within each constraint family (the source-disjoint 80/20 test split of Table~\ref {tab:verifier}, broken down by family; scores are those of the single in-distribution model). AUC 95\% CI: bootstrap over source items ($B=5000$). Viol.: share of test records that are oracle violations. A baseline that scores each record by its family's training-set violation rate reaches a pooled AUC of 0.759, against the verifier's 0.850. Labels follow the original stability rule; on the items the final rule scores, the stability AUC is 0.911 (Supplementary Note~2).}}{17}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:verifierfam}{{6}{17}{\justifying In-distribution verifier performance within each constraint family (the source-disjoint 80/20 test split of Table~\ref {tab:verifier}, broken down by family; scores are those of the single in-distribution model). AUC 95\% CI: bootstrap over source items ($B=5000$). Viol.: share of test records that are oracle violations. A baseline that scores each record by its family's training-set violation rate reaches a pooled AUC of 0.759, against the verifier's 0.850. Labels follow the original stability rule; on the items the final rule scores, the stability AUC is 0.911 (Supplementary Note~2)}{table.6}{}}
